ause that would directly remove that symbol). However if the same symbol appears elsewhere, we could remove one occurrence? But the phrase \"never remove ... a required symbol\" suggests the node itself is considered the required symbol, so it's not removable. But then we also have condition that after removal all required symbols must remain represented. That condition would be automatically satisfied if we never remove any node with required symbol, but we might remove a node with a non-required symbol that is the only provider of a required symbol? Wait, required symbols are symbols, not node ids. So if a node has a required symbol, removing it would remove that symbol unless another node with same symbol exists. So the condition \"never remove ... a required symbol\" might mean 'never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols' (permanent block). That would simplify: we only consider removing nodes whose symbol is not in required_symbols. Then we check if removing it would cause any required symbol to be missing (because the node might be the only occurrence of some required symbol? No, because we already excluded nodes with required symbols. So required symbols would only be on nodes we never remove. So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" would be automatically true. But then why include it? Possibly the required_symbols list includes symbols that must be present after reduction, but we might still remove a node that carries a required symbol if there is another node with same symbol. But the phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" is absolute. I'll assume the stricter: we cannot remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols. That matches typical grammar-guided reduction where certain symbols are non-terminals that must stay.\n\nBut look at the fixture: required_symbols = [\"root\", \"s1\"]. root is the root node's symbol, and s1 appears on n1 (depth1) and n4 (depth2). n1 is not removable (removable=false), n4 is removable. So n4 has symbol s1, which is required. According to \"never remove a required symbol\", we cannot remove n4 even though it's removable. That would mean n4 stays. But is that consistent with the overall objective? Possibly we want to remove as many removable nodes as possible while preserving required symbols. If n4 is the only node with symbol s1 (but n1 also has s1 and is not removable), so we could remove n4 because s1 still exists on n1. But the instruction says never remove a required symbol - ambiguous. The phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" could be interpreted as 'never remove a node that is required' meaning a node might have a field 'required' but that field doesn't exist. So I think it's about the symbol. Yet it says \"node with retained children\" also, so \"required symbol\" likely modifies \"node\" i.e., a node that has a symbol that is required. So n4 is such a node. So we cannot remove it. That means n4 stays. But then n2 (parent of n4) is removable. n2 has symbol s2 (not required). n2 is depth1, removable=true. Can we remove n2? n2 has children: n4. But n4 is retained (since we can't remove it). Then n2 has retained children, so we cannot remove n2 because \"never remove a node with retained children\". So n2 stays. Similarly n6 (depth2, symbol s0) is removable, parent n1 (not removable) - no children. n6 is not required symbol (s0 not in required). So we could remove n6 if all required symbols remain. Removing n6 removes s0, but s0 is not required. So allowed. n5 (depth2, symbol s2, not required, not removable). n3 (depth1, symbol s0, not required, not removable). So possible removal: n6 only? But also n4? No, n4 is required symbol. So only n6 is removable and can be removed. That would result in removed: [n6], retained: all others. But that seems too trivial. Maybe we can remove n4 because s1 also appears on n1? But the constraint says never remove a required symbol. So I'm leaning that we cannot remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols, regardless of duplicates. That makes the problem simple: only nodes with non-required symbols and no retained children can be removed. Let's test with fixture case b-000: removable nodes: n2 (depth1, symbol s2) - has children n4 (retained, symbol s1 required) so cannot remove n2 because it has retained children (n4). n4 (depth2, symbol s1) - required, cannot remove. n6 (depth2, symbol s0) - not required, parent n1 (not removable, but n1 has n6 as child; n1 is retained, but n6 has no children. Does n6 have any retained children? No children. So n6 can be considered. Check if all required symbols remain: root and s1 are on root and n1 and n4. Removing n6 doesn't affect them. So n6 can be removed. So removed = [n6], retained = all others sorted: [n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root] but root is \"root\"? The IDs are \"root\",\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\". So retained sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Sorting strings: \"n1\" < \"n2\" < \"n3\" < \"n4\" < \"n5\" < \"root\"? Actually \"root\" starts with 'r', which is > 'n', so \"root\" would come after all n's. So sorted retained: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. That seems plausible.\n\nBut wait, maybe we can also remove n4 if we consider that required symbol s1 appears elsewhere (n1). But the \"never remove a required symbol\" forbids it. However, perhaps the interpretation is: \"required symbol\" means a node that is marked as required (like a field). But there is no such field. Alternatively, \"required symbol\" might be the set of symbols that must be present after reduction, and we are allowed to remove nodes with those symbols as long as another node with the same symbol remains. The phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" is ambiguous. Looking at typical Perses node reduction: it tries to remove nodes while preserving a property (like the output is still interesting). The required symbols might be the set of non-terminal symbols that must appear in the parse tree? Actually the parse tree has terminals and non-terminals. Here symbols are like s0,s1,s2,root. Perses uses a grammar to generate variants. The \"required_symbols\" might be the symbols that are required to be present in the reduced tree (like start symbol and some others). In that case, you can remove nodes with those symbols if they are not the only instances. But the instruction \"never remove a required symbol\" might be a misinterpretation of \"never remove a node that is necessary to keep all required symbols represented\". The latter is already in \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\". So I think the \"never remove ... a required symbol\" is actually a restatement of that condition: a node that is itself a required symbol (i.e., the only node providing that symbol) should not be removed. But since we check the condition, we don't need the extra \"never\". Actually the text says: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This list: root (cannot remove), a required symbol (ambiguous), or a node with retained children. The phrase \"a required symbol\" might be a node that is required in the sense that its removal would cause a required symbol to disappear? But that's redundant with the later condition. Perhaps it's a node that has the \"required\" attribute set to true. But no such attribute. Given the examples, we need to decide.\n\nLet's inspect the fixture more: All nodes have removable boolean. n1, n3, n5 are not removable. n2, n4, n6 are removable. required_symbols: root, s1. So n1 and n4 have symbol s1. n1 is not removable, n4 is removable. If we can remove n4, then we would remove n4 and maybe n6. But n4 might have children? n4 has no children (depth2, no children listed). So if we remove n4, we check all required symbols remain: root is there, s1 is on n1, so okay. So we could remove n4. Similarly, n2 is removable and has child n4. If n4 is removed, then n2 no longer has retained children? But we process deeper first, so we would remove n4 first, then later consider n2. After removing n4, n2 becomes childless? Actually n2 had child n4, but n4 removed, so n2 has no retained children. So we could then remove n2 if it's removable and not required symbol (s2 not required). Then after removing n2, we consider n6? n6 parent n1, no children, removable, s0 not required, so can remove. So we could remove n4, n2, n6. But n4 symbol s1 is required; we removed it but there is still n1 with s1. Is that allowed? The condition \"never remove a required symbol\" would forbid removing n4. But if we interpret that as \"do not remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols\", then n4 is out. So we cannot remove n4. Then n2 cannot be removed because it has retained child n4. So only n6 removed. That seems too trivial. Also note that utilities are given, perhaps they are used to decide which node to remove when multiple candidates exist? The problem mentions \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"utility\". Possibly the reduction algorithm selects among removable nodes in order of something like utility? But the instruction \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" gives order. There's no mention of utility for selection, only tie-breaking with node ID. So I'd stick with deeper first, node ID tie.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I need to infer from the typical Perses approach. In Perses, reduction works by trying to delete subtrees (nodes) and checking if the resulting tree is still \"interesting\" (e.g., compiles). It does not have a concept of required symbols per se, but the grammar ensures validity. Here we have required_symbols, which might represent the set of symbols that must remain after reduction (like the start symbol and any tokens). The algorithm likely tries to remove as many nodes as possible while ensuring that for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains. In that case, we can remove any node, including those with