rent field (parent id). For root, parent is null.\n- Identify root by parent null.\n- Determine nodes that are removable (removable=True) and are not root.\n- Process nodes in order of depth descending, and for same depth, by node ID ascending (string sort).\n- For each node in that order:\n   - If node is already removed (in removed set), skip.\n   - Check if node has any retained children (i.e., any child that is not removed). If yes, skip because \"cannot remove node with retained children\".\n   - Check if node's symbol is in required_symbols and if after removal, there would be no other node with that symbol among retained nodes. Actually the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is broader: we need to ensure that for each required symbol, at least one node (not removed and not the current node being considered) has that symbol. But we are currently removing the node, so we check if after hypothetical removal, all required symbols are still present in the set of retained nodes (including nodes not yet processed but will be retained eventually? Since we are processing bottom-up, nodes deeper have been processed; nodes shallower may still be removed later. But the condition should be check against the current set of retained nodes (nodes not removed yet) minus the current node. That is the typical greedy deletion approach: if removal of this node would cause a required symbol to be missing from the remaining nodes (including those that may be removed later), then we cannot remove it. But since we might later remove nodes that also have that symbol, we need to be conservative. Typically in tree reduction, you only check if the required symbol appears elsewhere among the current set of nodes that are not yet removed (i.e., the set of nodes that will definitely stay?). But it's safe to check against all nodes that are not yet removed and not the current node, because later removals might remove that other occurrence. However if we later remove that other occurrence, then we would have already removed the current node and later might cause required symbol loss. To avoid that, the standard approach is to consider that once we decide to keep a node (because we cannot remove it), we never remove it. But we are processing in deterministic order and may remove nodes greedily. The condition \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" should be interpreted as: after removal of this node, considering all nodes that will remain (i.e., all nodes that are not removed and not scheduled to be removed later), do all required symbols exist? But we don't know future removal decisions. The safest is to use a conservative condition: require that after removal, for each required symbol, there exists at least one node (other than the current) that is not removable (i.e., removable=False) or that we have already decided to retain (because we didn't remove it). But that's complicated.\n\nGiven the simplicity of the fixture (only three removable nodes, one of which is required symbol), I think the expected algorithm is: remove all removable nodes that do not cause loss of required symbols, processing deeper first, and if a node has children that are already removed, it can be removed. This is essentially an iterative deletion of leaves that are not required. The utility might not matter. So my algorithm above (remove n4, n6, n2) seems plausible.\n\nBut need to check if n4 should be removed because it's a required symbol? The required_symbols includes s1, which n4 has. But there is an alternative node n1 with s1 that is not removable. So removal is okay. The \"never remove a required symbol\" would block this, but I think that phrase is poorly worded. I'll assume removal allowed.\n\nTo be safe, I'll look for any other clues in the problem description: \"registered_candidate_order\" might refer to the order in which candidates are generated. Perhaps the \"utility\" field is used to rank candidates: remove nodes with lower utility first? But it says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That is the primary ordering. Then \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So utility not used. I'll proceed without utility.\n\nThus solve(case) should:\n- Input: case (dict with keys: nodes (list of dicts), required_symbols (list), _case_nonce (ignore))\n- Build tree: map id to node, also map parent to children list.\n- Determine depth from input (given as depth field, but we can trust it).\n- Identify root: node with parent null.\n- Initialize removed_set = set(), removed_order = [].\n- Build list of nodes to process: all nodes except root? But root is never removed. We'll include but skip.\n- Sort nodes by (-depth, id) so deepest first, then id ascending.\n- Create a set of required_symbols for quick check.\n- Define a function to check all_required_represented(removed_set_excluding_current): For each req_sym, check if any node not in removed_set_excluding_current has symbol equal to req_sym. (We'll consider the current node as removed temporarily)\n- Iterate over sorted nodes:\n   - if node id in removed_set: continue\n   - if node is root (id == root id? we know root id is \"root\"? It could be any, but in fixture it's \"root\". We'll just check parent is None) skip (cannot remove root)\n   - if node['removable'] is False: skip\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and check_required_after_removal? Actually we need to check general condition, not only required symbol node. But we also need to check if node has retained children. For children, we can check if any child of this node is still in the tree (not removed). Since we process bottom-up, children are either already processed and possibly removed. So we can check if any child id is not in removed_set.\n   - if any child not removed: skip (cannot remove node with retained children)\n   - else: temporarily assume removal, check all_required_represented (with this node added to removed_set). If true, then actually remove: add to removed_set, append to removed_order.\n- After processing, retained nodes are all nodes not in removed_set.\n- Sort retained node IDs (string sort) and return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}.\n\nNote: We must not mutate case. So we work with copies of data.\n\nPotential edge: what if a node has multiple children, some removed, some not? Then cannot remove because still has retained children.\n\nNow implement in Python. Must be self-contained, no imports beyond standard library. Use json for output? Not needed; returning dict is fine.\n\nWrite solve(case) function. The case is a dict with 'nodes' list and 'required_symbols' list. Return dict.\n\nI'll produce the implementation code accordingly.\n\nBut before final, consider if utility is used: could be that we need to decide which of multiple removable nodes at same depth to remove first, but we already have ID tie. Utility might be a secondary tie? But instruction says \"node ID as stable tie break at equal depth\". So ignore utility.\n\nAlso note: \"registered_candidate_order\" might refer to the order in which nodes are listed in the payload? But not used. The instruction says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" So we must follow the order defined: deeper first, then ID. That's registered_candidate_order? Possibly.\n\nThus final algorithm.\n\nNow I need to output JSON with \"implementation\" key containing the source code as a string. I'll ensure proper escaping.\n\nLet's write code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2142,"completion_tokens":6562,"total_tokens":8704,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":6072},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":94},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20